\title{Notes}
\author{dhsorens}
\meta{toc}{true}

\p{
    I am interested, broadly speaking, in formal proposition and proof.
    From a high level, what tends to catch my attention is the nuance of going from an intuitive, abstract idea into something formal—whether it's a formal specification of a program, a formalized theorem statement, or even something simply written out as a program.
}

\p{
    Historically this has manifested as written work focused on the problem of (correctly) specifying and formally verifying [financial smart contracts](dhsorens-000A).
    But I continue to be interested in [software verification more broadly](dhsorens-0015), the ongoing work to [formalize mathematics](dhsorens-000Z), and other developments in using [proof assistants](dhsorens-000B).
}

\p{
    I recently started work on formally verifying [all things zero-knowledge](dhsorens-0020) on the Ethereum protocol, so my interests are broadening but are still rooted in the same topics.
}

